tionale": "New York's strong Democratic presidential lean is the clearest evidence available, and it points to a solid Democratic edge. With no polls, ratings, or market pricing for the state, the forecast holds near the fundamentals rather than moving on thin signals.", "watch": "The first credible general-election polls in New York, or a sharp national shift toward either party, would be the most likely reason to revise the margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "New York remains one of the most reliably Democratic states in presidential elections, and its partisan lean of roughly eight points Democratic sets the starting point for this open-seat contest. With the Republican incumbent term-limited, neither party benefits from incumbency, and no candidate-specific polling or expert ratings yet exist for the state.\n\nRecent headlines center on the 2028 Democratic primary, including Rahm Emanuel's early positioning and dipping popularity for Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, but primary jockeying says little about how New York will vote in the general election. Democrats enter as clear favorites on the state's fundamentals alone, though the absence of polling leaves meaningful uncertainty about the exact margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8251, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7779, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.769, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7679, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7809, "margin": 8.0}]}